New opt-in option `--copy-imports` (default off; `copy-imports = true` in a project file): the copying import `import X := Q` gives the importing module a copy of the module `Q`, named `X` (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/107). The declaration means what a nested `module X refines Q { }` means. Each class, trait, datatype, codatatype, newtype and abstract type of the copy is a type of its own, different from the type of that name in `Q` and in every other copy of `Q`; each function, constant, method and lemma has the definition that `Q` gives it. `import opened X := Q` opens the copy. The copy has the export sets of `Q`: the name `X` gives the default export set, and ``import X := Q`E`` or ``import X := Q`{E, F}`` makes it give the named sets instead. A module that refines the importing module gets a new copy at each copying import that it inherits, and cannot redeclare the name; through an abstract import, a copy is known only by its signature. A module that `Q` declares or imports in the ordinary way is not copied, a copying import inside `Q` is copied again, and an abstract import of `Q` stands in each copy for a module that may differ. A copy adds no proof obligations, and is compiled as a module of the target program. A copying import is rejected if `Q` is `replaceable` (`replaceable module cannot be copied by a copying import`), if `Q` has a declaration with an `{:extern}` attribute (`a copying import cannot copy module 'Q', because ... has an {:extern} attribute`), if `Q` has an export set with the name of the copy, or if it would make the module graph cyclic; a copy of an abstract module is abstract, so the importing module must be abstract. A library built with the option records it, and is loaded only by a command that also uses the option. With the option off nothing changes: `import X := Q` is the parse error it was.
